    "title": "Nvidia's potential new deal with OpenAI would revive a spooky tech-bubble habit, analyst warns",
    "text": "Nvidia's potential new deal with OpenAI would revive a spooky tech-bubble habit, analyst warns | Morningstar\nMorningstar Capabilities and Products\nCompany\nPortfolio\nTools\nSections\nMarkets\nFunds\nETFs\nStocks\nBonds\nInvesting Ideas\nFor Advisors\nHome\nTools\nPortfolio\nWatchlists\nScreener\nCompare\nChart\nRating Changes\nSections\nMarkets\nFunds\nETFs\nStocks\nBonds\nInvesting Ideas\nFor Advisors\nMedia\nHelp\nWhat’s New\nNotifications\nProducts for Investors\nAll Products and Services\nHome\nNews\nMarketWatch\nNvidia's potential new deal with OpenAI would revive a spooky tech-bubble habit, analyst warns\nNvidia's potential new deal with OpenAI would revive a spooky tech-bubble habit, analyst warns\nProvided by Dow Jones\nJul 28, 2026, 2:46:00 AM\nBy Hannah Pedone\nInvestors are fretting over a report that Nvidia could backstop an OpenAI data-center lease, in what would be the largest such arrangement yet\nNvidia is in talks to provide a $250 billion backstop that would allow OpenAI to lease an Ohio data center, according to the Wall Street Journal.\nAs investors question the value of heavy artificial-intelligence spending, the specter of vendor financing once again hangs over the technology sector.\nOn Sunday, the Wall Street Journal reported that Nvidia (NVDA) is in talks to guarantee $250 billion in financing that would allow OpenAI to lease a data center being built in Ohio. The Ohio facility could cost over $500 billion and would be the largest data-center project announced to date, according to the Journal.\nNvidia and OpenAI did not respond to requests for comment.\nEven though it remains to be seen how a hypothetical deal would be structured, Seaport Research's Jay Goldberg is cautious on this sort of arrangement.\n\"I don't know all the details or even how real this is, but if it does happen, I think it's hard to justify for Nvidia,\" he said.\nGoldberg noted that companies have previously \"tripped themselves up with financing customer purchases.\" He cited the telecom company Lucent, which engaged in vendor financing to boost sales in the 1990s. The decision \"essentially broke that company,\" he told MarketWatch.\n\"These kinds of financing usually don't end well,\" Goldberg added.\nShares of Nvidia lost 5% in Monday's trading.\nThe Nvidia-OpenAI deal looks even more unusual when considering the electricity required to power the Ohio data center. The project is located on a decommissioned uranium-enrichment site, and the campus would require 10 gigawatts of electricity.\nIn exchange for lower tariffs, Japan has agreed to invest $33 billion in a natural-gas power project that will be operated by data-center developer SB Energy, a subsidiary of SoftBank Group (JP:9984) (SFTBY). OpenAI is also an investor in SB Energy.\nThe backing by Nvidia would enable SoftBank to raise debt on more favorable terms - which would otherwise be challenging given how S&P Global has flagged OpenAI as a key credit risk.\nThe U.S. government will pay a fee to SB Energy to operate the plant, but the revenue will be divided between Japan and the U.S. - that is, until Japan has been repaid its $33 billion. From that point, the U.S. would keep 90% of the revenue, which would come from OpenAI, a future buyer of the energy.\nThe Trump administration has pitched the deal as a win for Americans and Ohio, despite growing pushback against the data-center buildout.\nCommerce Secretary Howard Lutnick said in a March post on X that the project was \"only made possible by President Trump's trade and tariff policy!!\" He added that it promises to \"create thousands of jobs, lower electricity costs for American families, and deliver energy infrastructure needed to power the AI economy.\"\nThe project is located on federal land in southern Ohio.\nThe deal would be the latest in a string of vendor financing agreements in the AI ecosystem, whereby a seller lends to a buyer of its products.\n\"Investor feedback late last night was a combination of this being the clearest/largest circular-financing headline we've seen so far and we're not entirely new to this circular-financing debacle,\" Jefferies technology strategist Jeffrey Favuzza said in a note to clients.\nNvidia already has committed to investments in OpenAI, and also amended some of the earlier terms of such arrangements. In February, the Financial Times reported that Nvidia was pulling out from a $100 billion agreement originally announced in September, and replacing it with a $30 billion equity investment.\nBroadcom (AVGO) and Advanced Micro Devices (AMD) also have partnerships with OpenAI to deploy their chips. Additionally, OpenAI has committed $300 billion to Oracle (ORCL) to secure data-center capacity, much of which Oracle will use to buy Nvidia GPUs. Meanwhile, Nvidia holds $2 billion in cloud provider CoreWeave's stock (CRWV).\nSee also: Nvidia's Jensen Huang has a high-stakes message for AI policymakers\n-Hannah Pedone\nThis content was created by MarketWatch, which is operated by Dow Jones & Co. MarketWatch is published independently from Dow Jones Newswires and The Wall Street Journal.\n(END) Dow Jones Newswires\n07-27-26 1746ET\nCopyright (c) 2026 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\nThe articles, information, and content displayed on this webpage may\ninclude materials prepared and provided by third parties. Such\nthird-party content is offered for informational purposes only and\nis not endorsed, reviewed, or verified by Morningstar.\nMorningstar makes no representations or warranties regarding the\naccuracy, completeness, timeliness, or reliability of any third-party\ncontent displayed on this site. The views and opinions expressed in\nthird-party content are those of the respective authors and do not\nnecessarily reflect the views of Morningstar, its affiliates, or employees.\nMorningstar is not responsible for any errors, omissions, or delays\nin this content, nor for any actions taken in reliance thereon.\nUsers are advised to exercise their own judgment and seek independent\nfinancial advice before making any decisions based on such content.\nThe third-party providers of this content are not affiliated with\nMorningstar, and their inclusion on this site does not imply any\nform of partnership, agency, or endorsement.\nPopular\nView All\n4 Stocks to Buy for the Long-Term While They’re Still Undervalued\nPlus, a deep dive on SpaceX’s bid to challenge US telecoms.\nDavid Sekera, CFA and Sarah Hansen Oct 19, 2026\n6 ETFs To Diversify Away From AI\nA difficult problem has an easy solution.\nDaniel Sotiroff Oct 19, 2026\nWhy This Vanguard ETF Is One of the Best\nVanguard Total International Stock is appealing for several reasons.\nZachary Evens Oct 21, 2026\nThe Best AI Stocks to Buy Now\nArtificial intelligence stocks continue to attract investors, thanks to industry momentum and appealing valuations.\nTori Brovet Oct 20, 2026\nThe Market May Be Too Pessimistic About This 4-Star Dividend Stock\nDespite a looming patent cliff, a strong drug pipeline and 4.1% yield give investors reasons for optimism.\nKaren Andersen, CFA Oct 22, 2026\nSponsor Center\nTransparency is our policy. Learn how it impacts everything we do\nRead More\nTransparency is how we protect the integrity of our work and keep empowering investors to achieve their goals and dreams.\nAnd we have unwavering standards for how we keep that integrity intact, from our research and data to our policies on\ncontent and your personal data.\nWe’d like to share more about how we work and what drives our day-to-day business.\nHow we make money\nWe sell different types of products and services to both investment professionals and individual investors.\nThese products and services are usually sold through license agreements or subscriptions. Our investment management\nbusiness generates asset-based fees, which are calculated as a percentage of assets under management.\nWe also sell both admissions and sponsorship packages for our investment conferences and advertising on our websites\nand newsletters.\nHow we use your personal data\nHow we use your information depends on the product and service that you use and your relationship with us. We may use it to:\nVerify your identity, personalize the content you receive, or create and administer your account.\nProvide specific products and services to you, such as portfolio management or data aggregation.\nDevelop and improve features of our offerings.\nGear advertisements and other marketing efforts towards your interests.\nTo learn more about how we handle and protect your data, visit our privacy center .\nHow we approach editorial content\nMaintaining independence and editorial freedom is essential to our mission of empowering investor success.\nWe provide a platform for our authors to report on investments fairly, accurately, and from the investor’s point of view.\nWe also respect individual opinions––they represent the unvarnished thinking of our people and exacting analysis of our\nresearch processes. Our authors can publish views that we may or may not agree with, but they show their work,\ndistinguish facts from opinions, and make sure their analysis is clear and in no way misleading or deceptive.\nTo further protect the integrity of our editorial content, we keep a strict separation between our sales teams and authors to remove any pressure or influence on our analyses and research.\nRead our editorial policy to learn more about our process.\nSite Index\nWhat We Do\nAll Products & Services\nOur Signature Methodologies\nMorningstar Investment Conference\nCompany\nAbout Us\nCareers\nCulture\nCorporate Sustainability\nNewsroom\nInvestor Relations\nGet Help\nAdvertising Opportunities\nFeedback\nReprints\nInvesting Terms and Definitions\nGlobal Contacts\nAffiliate Program\nUnited States\n© Copyright 2026 Morningstar, Inc. All rights reserved.\nDow Jones Industrial Average, S&P 500, Nasdaq, and Morningstar Index (Market Barometer) quotes are real-time.\nTerms and Conditions\nPrivacy Center\nDisclosures\nMember User Agreement\nCorrections\nDo Not Sell or Share My Personal Information\nAccessibility"
